hadiths[3]{hadithnumber,text}:
"2937","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Rafi’, narrated to us Yazid ibn Harun, informed us al-Hajjaj ibn Arta’ah, on the authority of Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad, on the authority of ‘Amrah, on the authority of ‘A’ishah (RA), who said:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, “When you have stoned [the Jamrah] and shaved [your heads], then perfume and clothing become lawful for you, except for marriage.”\nAbu Bakr said: His statement, “except for marriage,” means the marriage that refers to intercourse. And I had already mentioned in my book *Ma’ani al-Qur’an* that the word *nikah* among the Arabs applies to both the contract and intercourse.\n\nCHAPTER:"
"2938","Narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan, from 'Amr, who said: I heard Salim saying: 'A'ishah (RA) said:\n\I perfumed the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)"","" and the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) is more worthy to be followed.\""\n\nCHAPTER:"""""
"2939","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Rafi', narrated to us 'Abd al-Razzaq, informed us Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn 'Umar, from 'Umar (RA), who said:\n\When a man throws the Jamrah with seven pebbles"","" slaughters his sacrificial animal"","" and shaves his head"","" everything becomes lawful for him except women and perfume.\""\n\nSalim (RA) said:\n'A'ishah (RA) used to say: \""Everything becomes lawful for him except women"",""\"" and she said: \""I perfumed the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).\""\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said regarding the report of 'A'ishah (RA): \""I perfumed the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) so that he could be in a state of ihram before performing Tawaf around the House"",""\"" indicating that when one throws the Jamrah"","" slaughters"","" and shaves his head"","" he becomes lawful before performing Tawaf"","" except for what has been prohibited regarding intimacy with women. There is no difference of opinion among the scholars that he is prohibited from intimacy with women until he performs the Tawaf of ziyarah (the Farewell Tawaf).\n\nCHAPTER:"""""
